<speak>
<p>Assume there is a circulant Hadamard matrix of order four u squared, with u odd and greater than one. Let I be the set of distinct prime divisors of u. For a subset S, let zeta sub S be the product of its chosen prime roots, with the empty product equal to one. The preceding polynomials define one element, script S: the alternating sum of E at zeta sub S divided by c at zeta sub S.<break time="450ms"/></p>
<p>Two proof branches now meet at this same element. Node seven's product-to-sum argument gives script S residue zero modulo one plus i. Node eight point three's all-ones calculation gives script S residue one modulo one plus i. These conclusions come from the same assumed matrix and the same subset inputs.<break time="450ms"/></p>
<p>Both statements live in the same quotient ring, A modulo the ideal generated by one plus i. Here A is the integers with i, the selected prime roots, and one over u adjoined. Node six proved that this quotient is nonzero. Its zero and its identity are distinct. Every evaluated denominator c sub S is a unit before reduction, and remains a unit afterward. The normalized expressions are therefore defined in this one ring.<break time="450ms"/></p>
<p>Bring the two values of script S together. They would force zero to equal one. That is impossible in the nonzero quotient. Characteristic two makes one plus one equal zero; it does not make one equal zero. We have reached a contradiction to the assumed circulant Hadamard matrix.<break time="500ms"/></p>
<p>This excludes every odd u greater than one, rather than only the guiding case u equals three. The full subset calculation supplies the general conclusion. The argument does not exclude u equals one. The delta theorem used earlier requires u greater than one, so its odd-order conclusion cannot be applied at u equals one. The next step combines this exclusion with the order restriction and checks the two remaining matrix orders.<break time="500ms"/></p>
</speak>
